[Music]  foreign  Thousand Days China's borders have been  closed to the world and its population  has suffered through lockdowns  China's zero covert policy XI jinping's  response to the virus mostly kept the  pandemic at Bay saving lives but by the  end it had become a waking nightmare for  many residents attempting to cut metal  chains trying to break free from  lockdown in an eastern district of the  city officers were forcing people out of  their rented apartments that policy was  scrapped in early December since then  covid-19 has ravaged the population this  Shanghai hospital is one of the best in  the country and it's overwhelmed by the  current surge in covert cases right now  in China things look Grim tens of  millions of people are catching the  virus hospitals are overwhelmed more  than a million people might die as a  result of this latest outbreak see  sudden policy reversal will have deadly  consequences for the people of China but  as he opens the country for business  with the rest of the world his actions  will have a profound Global impact  China's reopening will be 2023's biggest  economic event I'm Rachel scharnberg The  Economist business Affairs editor and  here are my three key takeaways on what  China's reopening means for the world  economy  [Music]  China's economy suffered during the zero  covert policy and that was probably one  reason why the policy was increasingly  untenable for China's leadership some  economists think that by the first  quarter of 2024 China's GDP might be 10  percent bigger than it is expected to be  in the first three months of this year  outside of China reopening will have  positive and negative effects one thing  to look at is tourism  for countries that rely heavily on  Chinese tourism reopening will be very  welcome news tourists will start to go  out and about again and for a place like  Hong Kong that will mean a big boost to  GDP economists think it might Rise by  about eight percent once China fully  reopens although countries in the west  are starting to place travel  restrictions on Chinese tourists that  won't affect their economies as much as  they're less reliant on Chinese tourist  spending but what will affect the West  is another unfortunate side effect of  China reopening  China is a big consumer of the world's  Commodities it buys about a fifth of the  world's oil and three-fifths of the  world's iron all and as China starts to  grow faster it starts to demand more of  those Commodities which pushes up the  global price oil for example Goldman  Sachs a bank thinks that full Chinese  reopening might lead to prices rising by  about a quarter compared with today's  prices up to a hundred dollars a barrel  now that's not as bad as it was during  the spike soon after Russia invaded  Ukraine but it's still an extra  complication as central banks try to  fight inflation  there's an extra complication for Europe  which is trying to wean itself off  Russian natural gas that's meant relying  more on imported liquefied natural gas  and if China recovers very strongly it  might start to demand more LNG as well  that'll make it harder for Europe to  secure the supplies that it needs and  according to some forecasters in a  scenario where winter starts punctually  at the end of this year Europe might  find itself having to introduce  rationing  the Draconian implementation of  zerocovid and then the chaotic reopening  has had the effect of putting some  foreign companies off the idea of  relying so much on China for their  production some have started to shift  their operations elsewhere According to  some measures foreign investment into  new factories in China have fallen Apple  for example has shifted some of its  production out of China to Vietnam and  India partly because of some of the  chaos of operating during the final days  of the zero covered policy so perhaps  one of the big questions after China is  fully reopened is how quickly that trust  is won back and whether China manages to  woo foreign investors and business  people back again  watching to read more of our content on  China's reopening click on the link and  please don't forget to subscribe  [Music]

